    <string name="ob1">You are wintering alone at a polar station. The station is your body. Three times a day comes a synoptic term: you read the instruments and send a short report to the mainland.</string>
    <string name="ob1b">Bad days here are weather, not failures. Weather comes and goes; the observer stays at the post.</string>
    <string name="ob2_t">The instruments</string>
    <string name="ob2">Everything is measured on the real meteorologist\'s scales, and each term takes ten seconds. Energy is the wind, from calm to hurricane. Mood is the cloud cover in eighths of the sky.</string>
    <string name="ob2_a">A light breeze under a heavy sky: no strength, and gloomy inside.</string>
    <string name="ob2_b">A fresh wind, some cloud: an ordinary working day.</string>
    <string name="ob2_c">A strong wind and a clear sky: I could move mountains.</string>
    <string name="ob2_rest">Pain is precipitation over a map of the body: drizzle, rain or a thunderstorm. In the morning you also log your sleep. Coffee, workouts and pills go into the weather box as instruments.</string>
    <string name="ob2_rare">The station grows with every term you keep, not with good days. An aurora comes after a cyclone has passed.</string>
    <string name="ob3_t">Call sign</string>
    <string name="ob3">What should your station be called? The name goes at the top of every telegram.</string>
    <string name="ob3_terms">The terms are at 09:00, 15:00 and 21:00. You can move them in the settings, and a quiet notification will call you to each.</string>
    <string name="ob3_private">The journal lives only on this phone. The app has no internet access. A report for a doctor is made only when you ask for it.</string>
